{
  "wireMagic": "pb3",
  "pens": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wsWinStyles": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wpWinPositions": [ {
  
  } ],
  "events": [ {
    "tStartMs": 570,
    "dDurationMs": 1819,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "You know what a planet is, right?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2389,
    "dDurationMs": 2461,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "A big round thing that orbits a star."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 4850,
    "dDurationMs": 1240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Not so fast."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 6090,
    "dDurationMs": 5400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The surprisingly vicious debate over the planetary\nstatus of Pluto has given us a fascinating"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 11490,
    "dDurationMs": 3650,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "glimpse into what a scientific definition\nreally is."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 15140,
    "dDurationMs": 5260,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And perhaps the word planet is too vague to\nbe used as a scientific definition at all."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 25360,
    "dDurationMs": 2300,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We love to classify things."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 27660,
    "dDurationMs": 3520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Labels help us keep stuff organized in our\nheads."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 31189,
    "dDurationMs": 5071,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In science, categorization provides a fast\nand easy way to know the properties of a member"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 36260,
    "dDurationMs": 3700,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of a group just by knowing what group it\nbelongs to."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 39960,
    "dDurationMs": 5580,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Chemists group elements on the periodic table,\nthose groups exhibit similar chemical behavior"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 45550,
    "dDurationMs": 2739,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that reflect outer-shell electron number."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 48289,
    "dDurationMs": 6020,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Biologists group organisms by similar physical\ncharacteristics, and this taxonomy reflects"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 54309,
    "dDurationMs": 2611,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "genetic relationships."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 56920,
    "dDurationMs": 3160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Astronomers are all about space taxonomy."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 60080,
    "dDurationMs": 3040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We classify galaxies based on their shape,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 63120,
    "dDurationMs": 3400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "black holes based on how they feed and how they're oriented,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 66520,
    "dDurationMs": 6780,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "stars based on their color and brightness, and\nplanets by… well, by a set of criteria that"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 73300,
    "dDurationMs": 5580,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "has caused more tension and heartbreak than\nany made-up grouping scheme really should."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 79660,
    "dDurationMs": 6420,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Because a change in that scheme demoted Pluto\nfrom planet to not-planet."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 86080,
    "dDurationMs": 4460,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Today we’re going to settle whether this\nwas reasonable, and whether we should keep"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 90540,
    "dDurationMs": 2079,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the word “planet” at all."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 92619,
    "dDurationMs": 3671,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The definition of “planet” has changed\na lot."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 96290,
    "dDurationMs": 6460,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "If you were an ancient astronomer like say,\nPtolemy, the planets were the asteres planetai,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 102750,
    "dDurationMs": 1720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the wandering stars."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 104470,
    "dDurationMs": 4721,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "These included Mercury, Venus, Mars, Jupiter,\nand Saturn, but also the sun and the moon"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 109200,
    "dDurationMs": 4440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "- basically anything that moved relative to\nthe background stars."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 113640,
    "dDurationMs": 2560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Note that they did NOT include the Earth."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 116200,
    "dDurationMs": 5459,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This definition of “planet” was the most\nsensible classification for thousands of years"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 121659,
    "dDurationMs": 4351,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "based on our observations and understanding\nof the universe."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 126010,
    "dDurationMs": 1660,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But understanding improves."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 127670,
    "dDurationMs": 6040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In 1543 Nicolaus Copernicus published “On\nthe Revolutions of the Heavenly Spheres”"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 133710,
    "dDurationMs": 4510,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "which cast the Sun, not the Earth, as the\ncenter of the universe and the Earth in its"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 138220,
    "dDurationMs": 2610,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "proper place among the planets."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 140830,
    "dDurationMs": 4670,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This picture was cleaned up by the observations\nof Galileo and Brahe and the mathematical"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 145500,
    "dDurationMs": 2040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "models of Kepler and Newton."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 147540,
    "dDurationMs": 6760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Newton’s Laws of Gravity led to consistent\npredictability of the motion of the heavenly spheres."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 154800,
    "dDurationMs": 6180,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The solar system finally made observational\nand theoretical sense: there were now 6 planets"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 160980,
    "dDurationMs": 4190,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "orbiting the sun in perfect mathematical harmony."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 165170,
    "dDurationMs": 4460,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But new discoveries were about to mess up\nthis beautifully simple picture."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 169630,
    "dDurationMs": 2640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "See that’s the thing about science."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 172270,
    "dDurationMs": 4040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "No so-called fact is ever perfectly safe."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 176310,
    "dDurationMs": 4550,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Everything is subject to revision if evidence\nturns against it."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 180860,
    "dDurationMs": 3020,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That’s the beauty of the scientific process."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 183880,
    "dDurationMs": 3100,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Keep this in mind when we get to Pluto."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 186980,
    "dDurationMs": 3880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "New wandering stars were discovered in the\ncenturies following Newton."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 190860,
    "dDurationMs": 4790,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Uranus had been spotted many times throughout\nhistory, but was only identified as a planet"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 195650,
    "dDurationMs": 4320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "after William Herschel recorded its movement\nin 1781."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 199970,
    "dDurationMs": 6340,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That same motion almost perfectly reflected\nthe clockwork predictions of Newtonian mechanics."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 206310,
    "dDurationMs": 1350,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "\"Almost\"."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 207660,
    "dDurationMs": 6040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Slight deviations in Uranus's orbit betrayed\nthe existence of Neptune, which was discovered"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 213700,
    "dDurationMs": 5300,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "first in the mathematics and then with a telescope\nin 1846."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 219000,
    "dDurationMs": 4420,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Between these two discoveries, four other\nbodies joined the planets."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 223420,
    "dDurationMs": 6350,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "At the beginning of the 1800s Vesta, Juno,\nCeres and Pallas were all spotted between"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 229770,
    "dDurationMs": 3170,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the orbits of Mars and Jupiter and classified\nas planets."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 232940,
    "dDurationMs": 5540,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But astronomers kept finding more and more\nobjects in that vicinity, and eventually realized"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 238480,
    "dDurationMs": 3450,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that a new group was needed in our taxonomy."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 241930,
    "dDurationMs": 3600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So the class of asteroids came into being."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 245530,
    "dDurationMs": 5000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "You might argue that Ceres, Juno, Pallas,\nand Vesta were unfairly demoted from planet."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 250530,
    "dDurationMs": 6440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "After all, Ceres is 20% the diameter of Mercury,\nso it’s in the planetary ballpark."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 256970,
    "dDurationMs": 1570,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But where do you draw the line?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 258540,
    "dDurationMs": 2800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "How many asteroids should we then classify\nas planets?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 261340,
    "dDurationMs": 6130,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Or do we drop Mercury, which is, after all,\nonly 50% larger than our own moon and smaller"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 267470,
    "dDurationMs": 2210,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "than Saturn’s moon Titan."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 269680,
    "dDurationMs": 5330,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Our taxonomy had to evolve as our understanding\nof the solar system grew, and so the term"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 275010,
    "dDurationMs": 7300,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "planet was reserved for the now-familiar 8\n- Mercury through Neptune, although the definition"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 282310,
    "dDurationMs": 2480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of the word was still vague."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 284790,
    "dDurationMs": 2970,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This is where Pluto finally comes into the\nstory."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 287760,
    "dDurationMs": 4840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Even with the discovery of Neptune, the orbit\nof Uranus still appeared a bit off - at least"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 292600,
    "dDurationMs": 1360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in some calculations."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 293960,
    "dDurationMs": 6200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And so began a feverish search for yet another\nplanet to resolve this discrepancy."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 300160,
    "dDurationMs": 6410,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The search for the so-called Planet X became\nthe grail quest of Percival Lowell, businessman"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 306570,
    "dDurationMs": 5810,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "turned astronomer. He built the Lowell\nObservatory in Arizona with that single goal."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 312620,
    "dDurationMs": 5800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In 1930, 14 years after Lowell’s death,\nbut still powered by his observatory and his"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 318430,
    "dDurationMs": 3420,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "family’s fortune, Planet X was finally discovered."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 321850,
    "dDurationMs": 2470,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Or so we thought."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 324320,
    "dDurationMs": 5650,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Clyde Tombaugh, a 22 year old Kansas farm\nboy, spotted a moving speck of light in a"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 329970,
    "dDurationMs": 6070,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "series of photographic plates taken under\nthe guidance of Vesto Slipher, Lowell observatory’s"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 336040,
    "dDurationMs": 1250,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "director."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 337290,
    "dDurationMs": 4930,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Orbital calculations put the object beyond\nthe orbit of Neptune, and it appeared within"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 342220,
    "dDurationMs": 5900,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "6 degrees of one of Slipher’s predictions\nfor the location of the mysterious planet X."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 348520,
    "dDurationMs": 5640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Astronomer’s were expecting a planet - and\none was found roughly where they thought is should be"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 354160,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "- so perhaps it’s not surprising that Pluto\nwas hailed as a planet without due scientific process."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 360000,
    "dDurationMs": 2280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That’s not to say everyone agreed."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 362280,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The orbit of this new object was far more\nelliptical - stretched out - than any other planet."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 367560,
    "dDurationMs": 6840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It also seemed too faint to possibly have\nthe mass required to explain Uranus’s orbital discrepancies."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 375000,
    "dDurationMs": 4600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "By 1931, astronomers had figured out that\nthere didn’t need to be a ninth planet to"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 379600,
    "dDurationMs": 2900,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "account for Uranus’s strange orbit."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 382500,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But because Pluto was the only such object\nyet discovered at that distance, it kept its"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 388350,
    "dDurationMs": 2160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "classification as a planet."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 390510,
    "dDurationMs": 6050,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "New astronomy textbooks included distant Pluto\nand generations of students memorized 9 rather"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 396560,
    "dDurationMs": 1580,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "than 8 planets."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 398140,
    "dDurationMs": 2980,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Fast forward several decades."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 401130,
    "dDurationMs": 5790,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "With the advent of giant telescopes and digital\ncameras, we began to find more and more objects"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 406920,
    "dDurationMs": 3010,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that muddied the definition of “planet”."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 409930,
    "dDurationMs": 4100,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In the late 80s the first brown dwarf was\ndiscovered."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 414030,
    "dDurationMs": 5470,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "These giant orbs of gas aren’t massive enough\nto ignite nuclear fusion in their cores like"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 419500,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a true star, but still seemed too massive\nto be called planets."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 424220,
    "dDurationMs": 4270,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And yet some brown dwarfs orbit other, more\nmassive stars just like planets do."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 428490,
    "dDurationMs": 6530,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Our overly vague definition of “planet”\nleft these brown dwarfs in taxonomical limbo."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 435020,
    "dDurationMs": 5900,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Also through the 1990s more and more moving\nspecks were discovered within our solar system,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 440920,
    "dDurationMs": 1880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "beyond Neptune’s orbit."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 442800,
    "dDurationMs": 5949,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "They were mostly much smaller than Pluto, but\nappeared to form a belt of countless objects"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 448749,
    "dDurationMs": 5890,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "- what we now call the Kuiper belt - and that\nencompasses Pluto’s orbit."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 454639,
    "dDurationMs": 4841,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Pluto became to the Kuiper belt what Ceres\nwas to the asteroid belt."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 459480,
    "dDurationMs": 2010,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The biggest fish in the pond."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 461490,
    "dDurationMs": 2800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Big enough to cling to its title of planet."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 464290,
    "dDurationMs": 2310,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Or so we thought."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 466600,
    "dDurationMs": 5400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Telescopes got bigger and our mapping of the\nKuiper belt became more thorough, and in the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 472010,
    "dDurationMs": 6659,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "early 2000s a number of objects in the Kuiper\nbelt and beyond were found to be similar in"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 478669,
    "dDurationMs": 1741,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "size to Pluto."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 480410,
    "dDurationMs": 5770,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Quaoar, Makemake, Orcus, Sedna, Eris, and\nmore."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 486180,
    "dDurationMs": 2180,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Eris was the last straw."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 488360,
    "dDurationMs": 6720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It’s 28% more massive than Pluto, which\nspurred NASA to initially hail it as the tenth planet."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 496160,
    "dDurationMs": 6120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But theoretical predictions suggested that\nwe’d only seen the tip of the Kuiper belt iceberg."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 502280,
    "dDurationMs": 3900,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "There must be hundreds more objects\nin the mass range of Pluto -"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 506180,
    "dDurationMs": 2560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and perhaps a couple of thousand."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 508740,
    "dDurationMs": 6900,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "If we classed them all as planets, schoolchildren\nwould need a novella-length mnemonics to remember them all."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 515940,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And so, despite the anger of schoolchildren\neverywhere, it was in their own interests"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 520820,
    "dDurationMs": 2000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that astronomers decided to act."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 522820,
    "dDurationMs": 7210,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In 2006, the International Astronomical Union,\nthe governing body of all astronomy names,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 530030,
    "dDurationMs": 5590,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "designations, and definitions, met to finally\ndefine what it meant to be a planet in our"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 535620,
    "dDurationMs": 1280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "solar system."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 536900,
    "dDurationMs": 2020,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "A number of definitions were debated."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 538920,
    "dDurationMs": 3750,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "They eventually voted and agreed to the following."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 542670,
    "dDurationMs": 1450,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "A planet must:"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 544320,
    "dDurationMs": 5540,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "One - be in its own orbit around the Sun, not around\nanother planet like a moon."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 549860,
    "dDurationMs": 8540,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Two - Have sufficient mass to assume hydrostatic\nequilibrium, meaning be roughly spherical."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 558400,
    "dDurationMs": 5060,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And Three - Have \"cleared the neighborhood\" around its\norbit."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 563460,
    "dDurationMs": 4280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "While Pluto satisfies the first two, it doesn’t\nmeet the third requirement."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 567740,
    "dDurationMs": 5460,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Were it a proper planet, it would have collected or scattered all Kuiper belt objects in its orbit."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 573380,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "“Planet” got redefined to something that Pluto\njust isn’t."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 578670,
    "dDurationMs": 6720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In this same process the IAU created an entire\nnew class of object - dwarf planet - an object"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 585390,
    "dDurationMs": 6280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "which has its own orbit and is spherical-ish,\nbut not massive enough to clear its orbit."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 591670,
    "dDurationMs": 7290,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Pluto, Eris, Ceres, Haumea, and Makemake were\nall promoted to dwarf planet, and many similar"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 598960,
    "dDurationMs": 3040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "objects will no doubt follow."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 602000,
    "dDurationMs": 4320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "While this definition was meant to settle\nthe debate, some still argue that Pluto should"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 606320,
    "dDurationMs": 2570,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "be grandfathered in as a planet."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 608890,
    "dDurationMs": 6509,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Even the current NASA Administrator, Jim Bridenstine,\nis declaring Pluto a planet once more saying"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 615399,
    "dDurationMs": 2041,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "“it’s the way I learned it.”"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 617440,
    "dDurationMs": 5709,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Well, political appointees declare lots of\nthings and not everything we learn as kids"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 623149,
    "dDurationMs": 1811,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "is true."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 624960,
    "dDurationMs": 5310,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Fortunately the actual scientific process\nis more rigorous than that."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 630270,
    "dDurationMs": 5940,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Scientific definitions require careful thought,\nprecision, and broad, expert consensus, and"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 636210,
    "dDurationMs": 1330,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "are subject to revision."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 637540,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Sure, we could add a fourth requirement to\nthe definition of planet, like: 4: ignore"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 642660,
    "dDurationMs": 3950,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "all of the above if changing things makes\npeople sad."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 646610,
    "dDurationMs": 5160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But because we are all curious and open-minded\nindividuals here, perhaps it would be more"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 651770,
    "dDurationMs": 4730,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "fun to think about WHY this reclassification\nis so contentious."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 656500,
    "dDurationMs": 3050,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Why all the sentimentality?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 659550,
    "dDurationMs": 4730,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The reclassification from planet to dwarf\nplanet DOES seem like a demotion."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 664280,
    "dDurationMs": 4630,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Look, Pluto - I’m afraid you haven’t cleared\nyour orbit of debris this quarter."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 668910,
    "dDurationMs": 3000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We’re going to need to reevaluate your role\nin this taxonomy."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 671910,
    "dDurationMs": 7110,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Don’t think of this as a demotion - it’s\nmore of a ... reincentivizing horizontal pivot."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 679020,
    "dDurationMs": 2460,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That conversation would make anyone cry."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 681480,
    "dDurationMs": 6620,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But let me assure you - the 13 sextillion\nkg ball of rock and ice that is Pluto doesn’t"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 688100,
    "dDurationMs": 5460,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "have strong opinions about its own taxonomic\nstatus among a few classification-crazy bipeds"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 693560,
    "dDurationMs": 2270,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "several planetoids away."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 695830,
    "dDurationMs": 5420,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We perceive this reassignment as a demotion\nbecause we anthropomorphize everything."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 701250,
    "dDurationMs": 5510,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But with such powerful imaginations, can’t\nwe just reframe this?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 706760,
    "dDurationMs": 4730,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "As we’ve peered deeper into our universe,\nwe’ve realized that it’s full of weird,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 711490,
    "dDurationMs": 5980,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "beautiful, and important worlds, some we now\ncall planets, some not."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 717470,
    "dDurationMs": 5610,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "For example, the moons of Jupiter and Saturn\nare active worlds that may prove to be the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 723080,
    "dDurationMs": 3030,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "only other homes for life within our solar\nsystem."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 726110,
    "dDurationMs": 5099,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And Pluto itself proved far more interesting\nthan “just another planet”."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 731209,
    "dDurationMs": 6171,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We already knew about planets - but Pluto’s\ndiscovery revealed the existence of the Kuiper"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 737380,
    "dDurationMs": 6180,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "belt, which is of fundamental importance to\nour understanding of how our solar system formed."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 744040,
    "dDurationMs": 7300,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Pluto went from being the least of the planets\nto one of the greatest of a new class of object."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 751340,
    "dDurationMs": 2160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And what about the term planet?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 753500,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Despite our attempts at improving the rigor\nand usefulness of the term, there’s still"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 758140,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "some ambiguity."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 759140,
    "dDurationMs": 5580,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The inner, rocky planets are very different\nfrom the outer gaseous planets."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 764720,
    "dDurationMs": 1890,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Should they receive different names?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 766610,
    "dDurationMs": 5530,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "There’s no single perfectly-correct way\nto classify groups of objects."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 772140,
    "dDurationMs": 4780,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Taxonomy is, by nature, a somewhat arbitrary\nart."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 776920,
    "dDurationMs": 5631,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And the new IAU definition of a planet, while\nbeing more precise, was somewhat tuned to"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 782551,
    "dDurationMs": 5978,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a pre-determined desire - to eject Pluto and\nretain the 8 other planets."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 788529,
    "dDurationMs": 6091,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "A touch arbitrary perhaps, but the new definition\nis scientifically useful."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 794620,
    "dDurationMs": 5230,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The eight solar system bodies currently defined\nas planets certainly share plenty enough in"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 799850,
    "dDurationMs": 6950,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "common - similarities in the way they formed\nand in the way they behave that make it *useful* to"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 806800,
    "dDurationMs": 2500,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "categorize them under a single label."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 809300,
    "dDurationMs": 4690,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And like any aspect of the scientific process,\nthis is subject to revision."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 813990,
    "dDurationMs": 4921,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Maybe at some point in the future, as we learn\nmore about how different worlds form, astronomers"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 818911,
    "dDurationMs": 3049,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "will change the definition of planet again."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 821960,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The language we use to describe the universe\nbecomes more precise as we learn more about its nature."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 828440,
    "dDurationMs": 6700,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And anyway, the word “world” still applies\nto Pluto - and it's a rather more poetic label for"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 835140,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "one of the greatest dwarf planets in known\nspacetime."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 840180,
    "dDurationMs": 1640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "OK, some announcements."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 841820,
    "dDurationMs": 5740,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "First up, we're launching a Space Time discord\n- It'll be the perfect place to ponder and discuss"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 847560,
    "dDurationMs": 3779,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the fundamental nature of the universe, 24/7."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 851339,
    "dDurationMs": 3261,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It's open to Patreon contributors of all levels."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 854600,
    "dDurationMs": 2980,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "You guys know that we have a Patreon, right?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 857580,
    "dDurationMs": 2460,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Well, we'd love to have you join the conversation there."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 860040,
    "dDurationMs": 6020,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Discord will be a great place to get your cosmic questions answered by the\nsmartest people on the internet -"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 866060,
    "dDurationMs": 4580,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that is to say, other spacetime viewers - and sometimes\nby me."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 870640,
    "dDurationMs": 5320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We're also starting a newsletter that's open\nto everyone to make sure you get notified"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 875970,
    "dDurationMs": 5370,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "when there's a new episode or about the news\nand events - like special content or if I'm"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 881340,
    "dDurationMs": 1670,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "giving a talk somewhere."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 883010,
    "dDurationMs": 2490,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "To get on that, follow the link in the description."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 885500,
    "dDurationMs": 5170,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And talking about Patreon - today\nI wanted to give a special shoutout to David Boyer,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 890670,
    "dDurationMs": 2380,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "who's sponsoring us at the big bang level."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 893050,
    "dDurationMs": 5599,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "David, in honour of your invaluable help we're\nnaming a dwarf planet after you."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 898649,
    "dDurationMs": 5321,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Dwarf planet David is a frigid, lifeless ball\nof ice and rock half the mass of Pluto and"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 903970,
    "dDurationMs": 2530,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "orbiting at the outer rim of the Kuiper belt."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 906500,
    "dDurationMs": 3330,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It'll be discovered in the late 2050s, at\nwhich point it won't be considered remarkable"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 909830,
    "dDurationMs": 1350,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in any way."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 911180,
    "dDurationMs": 2190,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Except for the fact that it's yours, David."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 913370,
    "dDurationMs": 2469,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And that makes it special."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 915839,
    "dDurationMs": 6310,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So, last time we looked at the harsh realities\nof what it would take to terraform Mars."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 922149,
    "dDurationMs": 4541,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Long-story-short - it's sort of possible,\nbut is really insanely difficult."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 926690,
    "dDurationMs": 5220,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "There were questions, comments, and lots of\nideas for alternatives."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 931910,
    "dDurationMs": 4870,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Kazeshi wants to do O'Neill cylinders or rotating\nhabitats in space."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 936780,
    "dDurationMs": 4940,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Now, the argument is compelling: why would we want\nto go and live back down in a gravity world"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 941720,
    "dDurationMs": 2679,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "right after getting out of this one?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 944399,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That's a fair point."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 945399,
    "dDurationMs": 5281,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Because a rotating habitat or cylinder in space can\nproduce the effect of gravity for its inhabitants,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 950680,
    "dDurationMs": 5349,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "but without an actual powerful gravitational\nfield limiting our access to space."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 956029,
    "dDurationMs": 4901,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "These structures would be much, much smaller\nthan a planet and so presumably easier to protect"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 960930,
    "dDurationMs": 4250,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "from radiation and asteroids than the entire\nsurface of Mars."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 965180,
    "dDurationMs": 4490,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We'd also need far less material than it would\ntake to rebuild the Martian atmosphere."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 969670,
    "dDurationMs": 4480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Also they're super-science-fictiony, which\nis an added bonus."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 974150,
    "dDurationMs": 5129,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Kholdaimon and other would prefer to terraform\nVenus."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 979279,
    "dDurationMs": 6141,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "After all, it's closer in mass to the Earth\nand has an atmosphere - albeit a searing hot,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 985420,
    "dDurationMs": 1650,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "horribly acidic one."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 987070,
    "dDurationMs": 6840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Now, the main issues with terraforming Venus\nare 1) its day is several months long, and that's"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 993910,
    "dDurationMs": 4750,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a long time for any photosynthesizing life\nto survive without the sun,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 998660,
    "dDurationMs": 5900,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and 2) it has essentially no water, so we'd have to be bringing all of that in."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1004570,
    "dDurationMs": 2980,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Venus also has no geomagnetic field."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1007550,
    "dDurationMs": 5170,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The magnetic field that it does have comes from\nthe interaction of the solar wind with its super thick atmosphere."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1012720,
    "dDurationMs": 4620,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But presumedly, we'd need to massively alter and even reduce that atmosphere,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1017340,
    "dDurationMs": 3520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "if we want to terraform it, which would probably kill the field."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1021080,
    "dDurationMs": 6219,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "On the other hand, covering venus with floating\ncities still seems like a fantastic idea - but"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1027300,
    "dDurationMs": 2420,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that's more akin to paraterraforming."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1029720,
    "dDurationMs": 6339,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "For Mars I say, why build a sky if you can\nbuild a roof? - in the case of Venus, why build"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1036060,
    "dDurationMs": 2620,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a sky if you can build a sky-city?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1038680,
    "dDurationMs": 5269,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Speaking of roofs, a few of you point out\nthat the best Martian option may be underground"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1043949,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "cities."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1044949,
    "dDurationMs": 5210,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "A couple of meters of Martian rock above your\nhead is plenty to protect against space radiation"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1050159,
    "dDurationMs": 1311,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and micrometeorites."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1051470,
    "dDurationMs": 5250,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Again, this isn't really terraforming, but\nthose tunnels could be really extensive,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1056720,
    "dDurationMs": 4900,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and perhaps we could eventually build an underground world down there."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1061620,
    "dDurationMs": 6249,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Loren Husky has misgivings about my plan to\nbuild a swarm of self replicating robots to"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1067869,
    "dDurationMs": 5371,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "capture and toss comets into the inner solar system in order to terraform Mars, suggesting that"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1073240,
    "dDurationMs": 2299,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "this could, in some way, backfire."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1075539,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Lol."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1076540,
    "dDurationMs": 1700,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Lol indeed!"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1078240,
    "dDurationMs": 2029,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "I can't imagine what could possibly go wrong."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1080269,
    "dDurationMs": 7191,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It's not like any of the previous life-obliterating comet\nswarms because this one will be controlled"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1087460,
    "dDurationMs": 1880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "by mindless robots."
    } ]
  } ]
}
